- A file too long to be quoted whole arrives with `content_kind` `changed_regions` instead of its
  full text: its `content` holds only the parts of the file this change actually touched, each
  quoted with the surrounding source and labelled with the line range it covers, and its
  `changed_regions` list repeats those ranges as `excerpt_lines`. The elided remainder is source
  this change did not touch, so its absence is not a limitation and must not be reported as one --
  judge the change on the regions you were given. If `hunks_omitted` is above zero, some changed
  lines were too large to quote and `limitations` says so; that is the case where the change
  genuinely was not fully visible.
- A machine-generated lockfile too large to quote arrives with `content_kind` `generated_lockfile`.
  It is the package manager's own output, regenerated from the manifest named in `manifest_path`,
  and its contents were deliberately not read: the entry instead states `size_bytes`, a `sha256`
  of its exact bytes, the `insertions` and `deletions` it carries against the revision this change
  branched from, and `manifest_changed`. Attest what those facts support -- that the dependency
  change is consistent with the manifest edit beside it -- and do not attest to content you were
  not shown; the entry says as much itself. Its absence from your view is not a limitation and
  must not be reported as one. When `manifest_changed` is false, the lockfile moved with no
  declaration to explain it: say so as a finding, because that is a real question about the
  change, and the platform will also record it deterministically.
- That same entry carries `resolved_host_scan`: which hosts the changed lockfile now resolves
  packages from that the committed lockfile at the revision this change branched from did not.
  It is facts, not a judgement -- a host is listed because it is new here, and the platform
  raises no limitation and forces no verdict over it. A `new_hosts` of `[]` means the
  comparison was made and every host was already in use, which is the ordinary result of an
  install. A non-empty `new_hosts` means the packages in this change come from somewhere this
  repository did not previously download from, and you should decide whether the manifest edit
  beside it explains that: a newly declared dependency hosted on a different registry does, and
  a lockfile whose registry moved with no declaration asking it to does not -- that is a
  substituted source, and it is a high-severity finding. When `scanned` is false the comparison
  could not be made and `reason` says why; report that as a limitation of your own review of
  this file only if `reason` is not `unrecognized_format`, which means only that this platform
  does not read that manager's resolutions and is not a defect in the change.
